## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates the ability to design tenant-isolated logging architecture using Cloud Logging sinks, IAM least privilege principles, CMEK encryption, and automated validation in a shared GCP project multi-tenant environment.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant log sinks with resource and label-based filtering
- Least-privilege IAM roles for secure log export operations
- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) for log data protection
- Log-based monitoring and alerting for leakage detection
- CI/CD automated validation using synthetic tenant provisioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate tenant log isolation in CI pipeline
const tenantId = process.env.TENANT_ID;
const logs = readLogs({
  filter: `resource.labels.tenant_id="${tenantId}"`,
  timeRange: '1h'
});

// Verify no cross-tenant log contamination
const crossTenantLeaks = logs.filter(log => 
  log.labels.tenant_id !== tenantId
);

if (crossTenantLeaks.length > 0) {
  throw new Error('Tenant log isolation violation detected');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates expertise in designing tenant-isolated logging architectures—a critical security requirement in multi-tenant SaaS platforms where log exposure can lead to data breaches.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Logging Router with tenant-specific sinks using label-based filtering
- IAM Conditions implementing least-privilege access controls
- Data Loss Prevention API for automatic PII redaction
- CI/CD pipeline integration with synthetic tenant provisioning
- Automated rollback mechanisms for isolation verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// Conceptual tenant sink configuration
const tenantSink = {
  name: `tenant-${tenantId}-sink`,
  filter: `resource.type="cloud_run_revision" AND labels.tenant_id="${tenantId}"`,
  destination: `bigquery.googleapis.com/projects/${projectId}/datasets/tenant_${tenantId}_logs`
};

// IAM Condition for tenant access
const tenantAccessCondition = {
  title: `Tenant ${tenantId} Log Access`,
  expression: `resource.name.startsWith("projects/${projectId}/datasets/tenant_${tenantId}_")`
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical knowledge of multi-tenant isolation for analytics stacks on GCP, with particular focus on data access controls, encryption mechanisms, and network security under production load conditions.

## Key Concepts

- BigQuery Row-Level Security (RLS) and column-level access policies for fine-grained data isolation
- Dataflow worker pools with dedicated service account per tenant to enforce least privilege
- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) per tenant with envelope encryption for additional protection
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls to establish network boundaries and prevent data exfiltration
- Per-tenant observability with dedicated Cloud Logging sinks and alerting pipelines
- CI validation using synthetic tenants to continuously verify isolation guarantees

## Code Example

```javascript
// Terraform-like JS object to illustrate per-tenant config
const tenantConfig = {
  tenantId: "tenant-001",
  bigQuery: {
    datasetId: "tenant_001_analytics",
    rowLevelSecurity: true,
    columnPolicies: ["restricted_fields"]
  },
  dataflow: {
    workerPool: "tenant-001-pool",
    serviceAccount: "sa-tenant-001@project.iam.gserviceaccount.com",
    scopes: ["bigquery.readonly", "storage.readonly"]
  },
  encryption: {
    cmekKeyRing: "tenant-001-keyring",
    cmekKeyName: "tenant-001-cmek",
    envelopeEncryption: true
  },
  network: {
    privateServiceConnect: true,
    vpcServiceControls: true,
    ingressPolicies: ["allow-analytics-ui"],
    egressPolicies: ["deny-all-external"]
  },
  logging: {
    logSink: "tenant-001-logs",
    metricFilter: "tenant=tenant-001"
  }
};
```
